    "content": "**The bug:** Default arguments in Python are evaluated **once**, at function definition time — not on each call. So `items=[]` creates a single list that every call shares. Appending to it means previous \"requests\" persist across calls, so `collect(\"a\")` then `collect(\"b\")` returns `[\"a\", \"b\"]` instead of a fresh `[\"b\"]`.\n\n**Corrected version:**\n\n```python\ndef collect(item, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(item)\n    return items\n```\n\nUsing `None` as a sentinel and creating a new list inside the function guarantees each call without an explicit `items` argument gets its own fresh list. Callers who pass their own list still get the mutation behavior they expect.",
